  "findings": [
    {
      "description": "os.system called with an f-string that interpolates `directory` into a shell command. If `directory` contains shell metacharacters or spaces, an attacker can execute arbitrary commands (command injection). Prefer os.remove/shutil.rmtree with validated paths or subprocess.run with a list and shell=False.",
      "category": "command-injection",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "description": "subprocess.call uses `shell=True` with an f-string that interpolates `path` and `host` into a shell command. If either contains shell metacharacters, arbitrary command execution is possible (e.g., host/path from user-controlled config). Use subprocess.run with a list of arguments and shell=False, or use shlex.quote if shell must be used.",
      "category": "command-injection",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "description": "os.popen executes arbitrary user input as a shell command without any validation or sanitization. This is a direct command injection vulnerability and also executes with the application's privileges. Use subprocess.run with a list and shell=False, or a safe API for parsing user commands.",
      "category": "command-injection",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "description": "The code does no input validation, path handling, or error handling. `cleanup_old_files` executes `rm -rf` on a path derived from user-controlled `directory`, which could delete unintended files if the input is malicious or contains a symlink/relative path traversal.",
      "category": "data-loss",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "description": "Use of `os.system`, `subprocess.call`, and `os.popen` is deprecated/best avoided in modern Python. Prefer `subprocess.run` with `capture_output=True`, `text=True`, and a list of arguments.",
      "category": "best-practice-violation",
      "severity": "info"
    }
  ],
